  { q: 'Where does the White-necked Raven live?', a: 'The White-necked Raven (Corvus albicollis) is endemic to sub-Saharan Africa, confined to the mountains and highlands of eastern and southern Africa. Its range extends from Ethiopia and Eritrea south through Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, the DRC (eastern highlands), Malawi, Mozambique, Zimbabwe, and South Africa (primarily the Drakensberg and eastern escarpment). It is essentially a mountain species, occurring between approximately 1,000–4,000 m elevation in rocky highland habitats.' },
  { q: 'What habitat does the White-necked Raven prefer?', a: 'The White-necked Raven is closely associated with cliff faces and rocky outcrops in highland and montane habitats. Within this nesting requirement, it forages across a range of open habitats: moorland, highland grassland, the edges of highland forest, montane scrub, and agricultural land adjacent to its rocky cliff nest sites. It is essentially absent from lowland areas, densely forested terrain, and arid lowland zones.' },
