  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The White-necked Raven (<em>Corvus albicollis</em>) is confined to the highlands and mountains of eastern and southern Africa — from the Ethiopian Highlands south through the East African Rift system to South Africa's Drakensberg. It is a cliff-associated montane species, essentially absent from lowland Africa, West Africa, and Central African forest zones.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Distribution</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The White-necked Raven's range follows the mountains and highlands of eastern and southern Africa:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ethiopian Highlands:</strong> present throughout, including the Simien and Bale Mountains. One of the more accessible regions for observation.</li>
      <li><strong>East African Rift highlands:</strong> Kenya (Mount Kenya, Aberdares, Rift Valley escarpments), Uganda (Rwenzori foothills, western highlands), Tanzania (Kilimanjaro region, southern highlands, Rift Valley).</li>
      <li><strong>Central/Eastern DRC:</strong> eastern highlands (Kivu, Maniema) bordering Uganda, Rwanda, and Burundi.</li>
      <li><strong>Southern Africa:</strong> Zimbabwe (eastern highlands, Chimanimani Mountains), Malawi (Mulanje, Zomba), Mozambique (Chimanimani extension), South Africa (Drakensberg range throughout KwaZulu-Natal, Eastern Cape, Free State highlands), Lesotho (throughout).</li>
      <li><strong>Elevation:</strong> typically 1,000–4,000 m; most abundant at 1,500–3,000 m.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The species is absent from West Africa, lowland East Africa (coastal Kenya and Tanzania), and the Congo Basin rainforest.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Habitat</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The White-necked Raven is the most strongly cliff-associated of the African ravens:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Core habitat:</strong> rocky gorges, escarpments, cliff faces, and rocky outcrops in highland and montane terrain. Almost always associated with exposed rock for nesting.</li>
      <li><strong>Foraging habitat:</strong> highland grassland, moorland, montane forest edges, agricultural land adjacent to rocky terrain. Forages widely from cliff-nest base, exploiting a range of food sources.</li>
      <li><strong>Urban:</strong> in some highland towns (Nairobi, Addis Ababa suburbs, Lesotho towns), occasionally breeds on large buildings where natural cliff sites are absent.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

